        "text_DHqPPc": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<h2>Your mask creates an air pocket in front of your eyes. That air space is why you can see at all, your eyes are built to focus in air, not water.</h2>"
          }
        },
        "text_m6V4RF": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>But that air space and mask lens change how light bends, which means:</p><ul><li><strong>Objects look about 25% closer</strong></li><li><strong>Objects look about 33% bigger</strong></li></ul><p>This \"magnification effect\" is why many people feel over-corrected using their exact glasses prescription underwater. The water is already helping you see.</p><p>There's also a second factor: mask lenses sit farther from your eyes than glasses do. This distance can make prescriptions feel slightly different, especially stronger ones.</p><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Your land prescription might not be your perfect underwater prescription.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
